[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Software engineering takes the latter view of computing: it focuses on the computer as a problem-solving tool and uses computing as a means to design and implement solutions to general problems, rather than solely examining hardware design or proving theorems about algorithms.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "We can view computing in a similar light. We can concentrate on the computers and programming languages, or we can view them as tools to be used in designing and implementing a solution to a problem.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "Software engineering takes the latter view, as shown in Figure 1.3. Instead of investigating hardware design or proving theorems about how algorithms work, a software engineer focuses on the computer as a problem-solving tool.",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "We will see later in this chapter that a software engineer works with the functions of a computer as part of a general solution, rather than with the structure or theory of the computer itself.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ],
    "type": null
  }
]

