[
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Strategy for finding a reusable component: Build an inventory of components and form a matrix with the features of all past and planned projects, then identify features that are needed and common to more than one project to target for reuse.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "The Council created an inventory of components and formed a matrix with the features of all past  and  planned  projects. Then, each  feature  was  rated  in  terms  of  whether  it  had  been implemented and was still needed, had been implemented but was no longer needed, or had not been implemented but was still needed.Those features that were needed and were common to more than one project were targeted for reuse.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "role": "paragraph",
    "segment": "Guidelines and styles to aid in selecting a component for reuse: evaluate whether the component performs the required function or provides the needed data; assess whether minor modification is less than building from scratch; check that the component is well-documented and that you can understand it without inspecting implementation line by line; ensure there is a complete record of the component's test and revision history to be confident it contains no faults. Producers should also make components general with parameters and anticipate similar invocation conditions, and separate dependencies so changing parts are isolated from those likely to remain the same.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "guidelines for you. If you are a consumer for your current project, there are four key characteristics to check about the components you are about to reuse:\n - Does the component perform the function or provide the data you need?\n - If minor modification is required, is it less modification than building the component from scratch?\n - Is the component well-documented, so you can understand it without having to verify its implementation line by line?\n - Is there a complete record of the component's test and revision history, so you can be certain that it contains no faults?",
        "img_id": null
      },
      {
        "type": "source_text",
        "content": "On the other hand, if you are a producer of reusable components, you should keep in mind several things:\n - Make your component general, using parameters and anticipating similar conditions to the ones in which your system will invoke your components.\n - Separate dependencies so that sections likely to need change are isolated from those that are likely to remain the same.",
        "img_id": null
      }
    ]
  }
]

