[
    {
        "role": "paragraph",
        "segment": "Object-oriented testing differs from traditional testing in several ways: unit testing tends to be easier because objects are small, but integration testing becomes much more extensive due to the push of complexity toward interfaces between components; encapsulation, while beneficial, requires more extensive integration testing; and inheritance introduces additional testing needs when a subclass overrides or redefines inherited methods, or when other functions in the derived class must remain consistent.",
        "citations": [
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "objects tend to be small, and the Easier complexity  that  might  ordinarily  reside  in  the  component  is  often  pushed  instead  toward  the interfaces among components.This difference means that unit testing is less difficult, but integration testing must be much more extensive.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "encapsulation is often considered a positive attribute of object-oriented design, but it also requires more extensive integration testing.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "when a subclass replaces an inherited method with a locally defined method with the same name, the overriding subclass must be retested, and probably with a different set of test data.",
                "img_id": null
            },
            {
                "type": "source_text",
                "content": "Similarly, inheritance introduces the need for more testing.An inherited function needs additional testing if Figure 8.16 depicts Graham's view of these differences.",
                "img_id": null
            }
        ]
    }
]

